09375 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-82\" transform=\"translate(142.015625 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_16\"><g style=\"fill: #333333\" transform=\"translate(327.233864 14.928) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><defs><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-86\" d=\"M 2362 4806 Q 2605 4806 2797 4755 Q 2989 4704 3142 4614 Q 3296 4525 3414 4416 Q 3533 4307 3610 4186 Q 3622 4160 3625 4131 Q 3629 4102 3590 4083 L 3226 3936 Q 3194 3917 3168 3933 Q 3142 3949 3130 3974 Q 2989 4141 2813 4243 Q 2637 4346 2368 4346 Q 1907 4346 1609 4122 Q 1312 3898 1171 3510 Q 1030 3123 1030 2637 Q 1030 2534 1049 2502 Q 1069 2470 1146 2509 Q 1389 2630 1670 2732 Q 1952 2835 2330 2835 Q 2822 2835 3171 2652 Q 3520 2470 3702 2156 Q 3885 1843 3885 1446 Q 3885 998 3664 656 Q 3443 314 3068 128 Q 2694 -58 2234 -58 Q 1670 -58 1264 220 Q 858 499 640 1027 Q 422 1555 422 2304 Q 422 3085 656 3645 Q 890 4205 1325 4505 Q 1760 4806 2362 4806 L 2362 4806 z\nM 2259 2387 Q 2093 2387 1885 2345 Q 1677 2304 1491 2240 Q 1306 2176 1171 2093 Q 1094 2042 1068 1987 Q 1043 1933 1043 1818 Q 1043 1587 1116 1340 Q 1190 1094 1334 876 Q 1478 659 1702 524 Q 1926 390 2234 390 Q 2534 390 2768 524 Q 3002 659 3139 896 Q 3277 1133 3277 1421 Q 3277 1690 3155 1910 Q 3034 2131 2803 2259 Q 2573 2387 2259 2387 L 2259 2387 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/></defs><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-82\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-86\" transform=\"translate(59.90625 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-a9\" transform=\"translate(126 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-82\" transform=\"translate(146.90625 0)\"/></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"pc2e3b29858\"><rect x=\"25.783281\" y=\"9.768\" width=\"345.96\" height=\"149.688\"/></clipPath></defs></svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · EBITDA bridge ($M)","note":null},"answer":"<p><b>Fill to 80%:</b> +60 students × $2,400 = +$144k per school; × 40 = <b>+$5.76M</b>.</p> <p><b>Fees +5%:</b> $150 more × 480 students = +$72k per school; × 40 = <b>+$2.88M</b>.</p> <p><b>10 new schools</b> at mature economics ($524k each): <b>+$5.24M</b>, needing $20M of capital.</p> <p>Year-5 EBITDA: <b>$26.2M</b>, about 2.1× today.</p>","note":"Fixed costs mean a 14% rise in students lifts school profit by 47%."},{"q":"What return would the fund make?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Entry: 9 × $12.3M = $111M; debt $49M; equity <b>$62M</b>.</p> <p>Exit at 9×: $236M; less $30M debt: $206M. Return: <b>3.3×</b> (about 27% a year).</p>"},{"q":"Which lever would you trust least, and why?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p><b>New schools</b>: they take time to fill, need good sites and teachers, and use cash. The plan assumes they're mature by year 5. <b>Fees</b> are the easiest to deliver but must be backed by visible quality, or families will switch. <b>Filling schools</b> is the biggest lever and fully in management's control, so start there.</p>"}],"reco":"Back the deal. Banyan earns $12.3M EBITDA today; filling schools from 70% to 80% adds $5.8M, a 5% fee rise adds $2.9M, and 10 new schools add $5.2M, taking EBITDA to about $26M in five years and the fund's return to about 3.3×. Filling existing schools is the biggest and most controllable lever, so put the first 100 days there. Risk: new schools may fill more slowly than planned. Next step: rank schools by utilisation and test marketing in the five emptiest.","great":["Builds school-level economics before company totals","Separates the levers in an EBITDA bridge","Explains operating leverage from fixed costs","Judges which lever is most reliable"],"id":69,"slug":"banyan-language-schools"}